{
  "id": "preserve-the-two-timedelta-precedence-rules",
  "title": "Preserve the two timedelta precedence rules",
  "kind": "rule",
  "answers": [
    "Which setting wins when ser_json_temporal and ser_json_timedelta are both supplied?",
    "Does explicitly configuring ISO temporal output override float durations?",
    "How do temporal_mode and timedelta_mode interact in to_jsonable_python?"
  ],
  "body": "Invariant: Dictionary configuration gives ser_json_temporal precedence whenever the key is present, including an explicit iso8601 value; otherwise timedeltas use ser_json_timedelta.\nEnforcement: pydantic-core/src/serializers/config.rs:TemporalMode::timedelta_from_config centralizes this presence check and is used by SerializationConfig::from_config and pydantic-core/src/serializers/type_serializers/timedelta.rs:TimeDeltaSerializer::build, keeping inferred and typed timedelta serialization aligned.\nArgument exception: pydantic-core/src/serializers/config.rs:SerializationConfig::from_args instead uses timedelta_mode when temporal_mode equals iso8601; any other temporal_mode takes precedence for timedeltas.\nConsequence: Replacing the configuration presence check with a value comparison changes explicit ISO overrides; applying the presence-based rule to argument resolution removes its default-mode fallback.",
  "applies": "Changes to timedelta mode resolution across dictionary configuration, serializer construction and direct serialization arguments.",
  "tags": [
    "serialization",
    "configuration",
    "precedence",
    "timedelta"
  ],
